course: dictionary
lesson: 4901
title: Illogical: when the reasons do not add up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Illogical means not making sense, or not following the rules of good reasoning. The root word is logical, which means sensible and well reasoned, so illogical is its opposite.
explain: The prefix il- means not, just as it does in illegal. The dictionary tells us «il- is used before l (illegal, illogical)», so the l in logical is why this word starts with il-. Look closely and you will see two l letters together: il + logical.
explain: Illogical can describe a step in a maths sum, a rule, or a feeling with no good reason behind it. «an illogical fear» is a fear you cannot explain with any reason. In Star Trek, Mr Spock says «Illogical» when someone's thinking is muddled.
fact: Illogical means not making sense, or not following good reasoning.
fact: Logical means sensible and well reasoned, so illogical means the opposite.
fact: The prefix il- means not, and it is used before the letter l.
fact: The adverb is illogically, which tells how something is done: «She argued illogically».
fact: Useful phrases: «totally illogical», «it is illogical to», «an illogical fear».
exercise: Mr Spock in Star Trek says «Illogical» when someone's thinking is muddled. What does illogical mean? | Full of colour | Very loud | Very tired | *Not making sense
exercise: Which phrase describes a feeling with no good reason behind it? | a happy birthday party | a bright red kite | *an illogical fear | a cold glass of milk
exercise: The prefix il- in illogical means what? | very | *not | again | under
test: Which word has the same prefix il- as illogical? | irregular | impossible | unhappy | *illegal
test: Which sentence uses illogical correctly? | The blue bench is very illogical to sit on | The bus was illogical and green | I felt illogical after my lunch | *It is illogical to say that two plus two makes five
exam: Ruby argued illogically, so nobody could follow her reasons. Which word tells how she argued? | *illogically | happily | quickly | loudly
